INT. DEPARTMENT STORE - PERFUME COUNTER - DAY

Glass cases. Gilt mirrors. A faint cloud of mixed scents hangs in the air.

MISS PRISCILLA ODUYA-FAIRBANKS, 40s, saleslady in a white smock, arranges tiny bottles with surgical precision.

Across the counter, MR. LEOPOLD KOWALCZYK-ADEBAYO, 50s, wearing a bowler hat, lifts a flask and sniffs it suspiciously. Next to him, his wife MRS. GENEVIEVE KOWALCZYK-ADEBAYO, 40s, in a feathered hat, glares at the saleslady. Their teenage son OSWIN, 15, hangs behind them, bored.

A floorwalker, MR. THEODORE ACHTERBERG-NWOSU, 60s, with a carnation in his lapel, hovers nearby, wringing his hands.

LEOPOLD
This smells like a wet dog.

PRISCILLA
That is our finest civet, sir.

GENEVIEVE
Leopold, put it down.

LEOPOLD
I like it.

GENEVIEVE
You like everything that offends me.

OSWIN
Can we go?

THEODORE
Madam, sir, perhaps the rose water--

LEOPOLD
No rose water!

GENEVIEVE
^I'll have the rose water.

Priscilla sets two bottles on the counter. Leopold and Genevieve both grab for the same one. They tug. The flask flies out of their hands, bounces off Oswin's shoulder, and crashes to the floor in a spray of glass and scent.

Everyone freezes. A rolling fog of lavender drifts across the shop.

OSWIN
(deadpan)
Smells like a wet dog.

Theodore faints. Priscilla catches him. Leopold throws his bowler hat in the air.

LEOPOLD
I'll take it!
